        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.049,
            0.847,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Another Peek Beyond the Point"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.088,
            0.316,
            0.113
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.122,
            0.847,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "1. Recall that a tenth is 0.1, a hundredth is 0.01, and so on. Find the following products in tenths, hundredths and so on: (a) 6 × 4 tenths = 24 tenths (b) $7 \\times 0 . 3$ (c) $9 \\times 5$ hundredths"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.24,
            0.373,
            0.335
          ],
          "content": "2. Find the products: (a) $2 7 . 3 4 \\times 6$ (b) $4 . 2 3 \\times 3 . 7$ (c) $0 . 4 3 2 \\times 0 . 2 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.34,
            0.847,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "3. Thejus needs 1.65 m of cloth for a shirt. How many metres of cloth are needed for 3 shirts?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.384,
            0.847,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "4. Meenu bought 4 notebooks and 3 erasers. The cost of each book was ₹15.50 and each eraser was ₹2.75. How much did she spend in all?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.427,
            0.848,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": "5. The thickness of a rupee coin is 1.45 mm. What is the total height of the cylinder formed by placing 36 rupee coins one over the other? Write the answer in centimeters."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.49,
            0.803,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": "6. The price of 1 kg of oranges is ₹56.50. What is the price of 2.250 kg of oranges? Can we write 56.50 as 56.5 and 2.250 as 2.25 and multiply? Will we get the same product? Why?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.484,
            0.904,
            0.547
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.553,
            0.847,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "7. Dwarakanath purchases notebooks at a wholesale price of ₹23.6 per piece and sells each notebook at ₹30/-. How much profit does he make if he sells 50 books in a week?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.616,
            0.608,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": "8. Given that $18 \\times 12 = 216$ find the products: (a) $1 8 \\times 1 . 2$ (b) $1 8 \\times 0 . 1 2$ (c) $1 . 8 \\times 1 . 2$ (d) $0 . 1 8 \\times 0 . 1 2$ (e) $0 . 0 1 8 \\times 0 . 0 1 2$ (f) $1 . 8 \\times 1 2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.716,
            0.716,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": "In which of the cases above is the product less than 1?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.74,
            0.847,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": "9. In which of the following multiplications is the product less than 1? Can you find the answer without actually doing the multiplications? (a) $7 \\times 0 . 6$ (b) $0 . 7 \\times 0 . 6$ (c) $0 . 7 \\times 6$ (d) $0 . 0 7 \\times 0 . 0 6$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.834,
            0.846,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "10. Multiplying the following numbers by 10, 100 and 1000 to complete the table."
        },
        {
